WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.750
Today I want to talk about
my struggles with Korean.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:07.500
I want to talk a bit about how I
have learned different languages,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.340
what the process has been for
me, where I have had success and

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.500
why, and where I have struggled.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.370
Some of the languages that I
have struggled in include Korean,

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.140
Arabic, and I'm going to go into why.
Today I'm gonna talk about Korean.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.380
My initial exposure
to Korean was in 1972.

00:00:25.555 --> 00:00:26.814
That's a long time ago.

00:00:27.115 --> 00:00:29.604
My wife and I were living in Japan.

00:00:29.634 --> 00:00:31.825
We went to Korea on a
visit just to see it.

00:00:31.854 --> 00:00:35.665
I was very interested in a
country on the continent of Asia

00:00:35.754 --> 00:00:41.485
with some kind of language relationship
to Japanese in terms of being of North

00:00:42.055 --> 00:00:47.155
Asia origin in its basic structure, an agglutinative
language, and, of course, with

00:00:47.155 --> 00:00:52.135
an awful lot of boring from Chinese in
the case of both Korean and Japanese.

00:00:52.425 --> 00:00:55.785
And of course, tremendous
cultural borrowing from China.

00:00:55.845 --> 00:00:57.075
So I was very interested.

00:00:57.075 --> 00:01:01.155
Had a great visit when Korea was,
I guess, considered a developing

00:01:01.155 --> 00:01:05.895
country, but it was really developing
as we now know. I went again in 1976.

00:01:05.895 --> 00:01:08.205
A good friend was at the
Canadian Embassy there.

00:01:08.205 --> 00:01:09.135
He was getting married.

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:11.085
He wanted me to be best
man at his wedding.

00:01:11.145 --> 00:01:13.125
He married a Korean lady, so I did that.

00:01:13.515 --> 00:01:18.165
At that time, I bought some books for
learning Korean, and I'm gonna show you

00:01:18.165 --> 00:01:20.385
my library of books for learning Korean.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.620
Yeah, but I didn't do much with it.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.470
I went back to Japan, where
again, I was living at the time.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.420
And then in 1980 I visited Korea.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.430
I was working for Atomic Energy of Canada.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:33.510
I thought I was gonna be very much
involved with Korea because Canada

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.880
was building a nuclear reactor in Korea.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.790
But in the end, I only spent one
year with Atomic Energy of Canada.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.800
Then I went back to the forest industry.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.320
And so again, my initial sort of spurt
of Korean study kind of went down the

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.180
tubes, but still it was out there.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:54.970
And then, you may know that in 2003
I wrote my book, The Linguist, about my

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:59.355
experiences in Learning nine Languages,
which at that time, didn't include

00:01:59.355 --> 00:02:01.365
Korean, and then we got started with LingQ.

00:02:01.395 --> 00:02:04.215
At any rate, I said, "You know
what? I should get back to Korean!"

00:02:04.485 --> 00:02:05.955
So I bought some more books.

00:02:06.015 --> 00:02:09.825
We didn't have Korean at LingQ. One
book, which was very good, which I

00:02:09.825 --> 00:02:12.915
really enjoyed was the Introduction
to Korean, I think it's called.

00:02:12.945 --> 00:02:16.485
You'll see when I'll show
you my library. Done here at the

00:02:16.485 --> 00:02:17.775
University of British Columbia.

00:02:18.135 --> 00:02:19.095
The narrators'

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.120
voices were so good, and
that's so important in any

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:23.470
kind of learning material.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:28.300
You can't have these beginner books with
a boring narrator. It just turns me off.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:33.550
And that was kind of the high
point of my enthusiasm for Korean.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.680
Also, the book was full of patterns.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.510
Language learning is about patterns.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:43.000
They had grammatical explanations, they
had terms like copulative verbs. Strange.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.670
But I ignored all those explanations.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:46.060
I just

00:02:46.355 --> 00:02:50.255
accepted the patterns, listened to the
patterns, listened to the dialogues,

00:02:50.495 --> 00:02:54.845
which were kind of semi-humorous
dialogues. Students situations that I

00:02:54.845 --> 00:02:56.585
was kind of more or less familiar with.

00:02:56.585 --> 00:03:00.545
Not too heavy on the sort of cultural
traditions and that kind of thing,

00:03:00.545 --> 00:03:03.545
which is more difficult because they're
less familiar. And that was kind

00:03:03.545 --> 00:03:06.815
of, as I say, the high point of
my enthusiasm for learning Korean.

00:03:06.975 --> 00:03:10.964
So I bought more books, but more and
more of these books, they were all so boring.

00:03:10.994 --> 00:03:15.165
And so I started losing interest.
And at that time, instead I became

00:03:15.165 --> 00:03:18.015
interested in Russian and we had
Russian at LingQ. And pretty soon Korean

00:03:18.644 --> 00:03:23.084
was in the rear view mirror and I
was spending all my time on Russian.

00:03:23.084 --> 00:03:24.495
I spent a lot of time on Russian.

00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:28.515
There was very interesting content,
political content, literature,

00:03:28.545 --> 00:03:30.704
audio books, so much good content.

00:03:30.704 --> 00:03:33.225
We had it at LingQ and so I was off with

00:03:33.405 --> 00:03:37.155
learning Russian. And then
Czech and then other languages.

00:03:37.515 --> 00:03:43.454
Although I have made regular attempts
to sort of restart my Korean, and

00:03:43.454 --> 00:03:48.315
you'll see here when I show you my
statistics that 2010 at various times

00:03:48.315 --> 00:03:51.704
I've said, "Okay, I should put in three
months, you know, a 90-day challenge."

00:03:51.704 --> 00:03:56.084
See what I can do to get my
Korean up to a better level.

00:03:56.234 --> 00:03:58.185
And I think I have improved.

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:02.280
Or did improve, but then of course I
didn't use it for the longest time.

00:04:02.369 --> 00:04:06.119
I'll show you a video at the end here
of me speaking Korean with my tutor

00:04:06.119 --> 00:04:08.549
after one of my 90-day challenges.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:12.540
But still, it was difficult at
LingQ for a variety of reasons.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.279
You know, we had some beginner content.

00:04:14.285 --> 00:04:15.510
We didn't have the Mini Stories.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.209
The intermediate content
was quite uninteresting.

00:04:18.214 --> 00:04:19.500
What I attempted to do

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:25.440
was find podcasts. And then I actually paid
someone to transcribe these podcasts so

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.119
that I could work my way through these.

00:04:27.179 --> 00:04:28.559
But they were very difficult.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:32.760
One was a literary podcast,
the other was a political podcast.

00:04:32.969 --> 00:04:34.679
I just fought my way through these.

00:04:34.679 --> 00:04:36.479
It was so hard.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:37.920
So many unknown words.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:41.370
And of course in Korean there's so many
little words that have eight possible

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.150
meanings and I didn't know which meaning
was appropriate, and we didn't yet have

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:49.080
Sentence View as we now have, where I
could get a sentence translation, which

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:54.090
would help me decide which of the sort of
translations of that word was accurate.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.380
I'll show you how I go through Korean
on LingQ today, and you'll see that.

00:04:58.675 --> 00:05:03.385
There are more sort of user hints from
other users who have looked up words

00:05:03.415 --> 00:05:07.315
and I can quickly see the most likely
translation of a word and looking

00:05:07.315 --> 00:05:10.735
at the sentence, meaning I can get
a better sense of what it means.

00:05:11.005 --> 00:05:13.105
None of that was available before.

00:05:13.165 --> 00:05:17.125
LingQ was slow, slower before, it
was just hard going. So there were

00:05:17.445 --> 00:05:18.645
too many headwinds.

00:05:18.645 --> 00:05:19.935
It was just too difficult.

00:05:19.965 --> 00:05:22.575
So I was always attracted
away to something else.

00:05:22.815 --> 00:05:26.685
So, you know, again, thinking back to
that sort of high point of my enthusiasm

00:05:26.865 --> 00:05:31.365
for Korean, there is that quotation
from Shakespeare's Julius Caesar where

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:36.104
Brutus says, "There is a tide in the
affairs of men, which if taken at the

00:05:36.104 --> 00:05:38.414
flood, will lead to success," or something.

00:05:38.414 --> 00:05:44.594
So if I had continued when I was most
enthusiastic, and if I had had less

00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:46.995
difficulty, or if there had been more

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:52.020
interesting content available, or
if LingQ had worked better, or if the

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:56.039
dictionaries were better, if all of
these conditions, if the attraction

00:05:56.039 --> 00:05:59.880
had been greater and the resistance
had been less, then I think I would've

00:05:59.885 --> 00:06:02.670
continued and achieved a better level.

00:06:02.675 --> 00:06:08.030
However, all the effort that I
have put into Korean is not lost.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:13.100
I have, according to my statistics,
some 40-, 50,000 known words,

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:15.440
which theoretically is a lot.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:19.460
Now, in all of these languages,
it depends on how we count words.

00:06:19.465 --> 00:06:22.790
There are a lot of different
endings to words in Korean that,

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.570
you know, help define the function
of the word in a sentence.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:31.130
So it's not something we can compare
to other languages. But I have a large

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:33.920
base of, you know, vocabulary.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:39.030
And even of comprehension. And my Chinese
helps, of course. So that if I were to go

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.340
to Korea...but I don't have the freedom.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.150
I have a family.

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.170
I have other things that I'm doing.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:46.680
I can't just go off to Korea for
two months and be with people

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:47.820
and speak Korean all the time.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:51.450
But if I were to do that, I think I
could bring it up to a decent level.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.460
Nevertheless, I am disappointed.

00:06:53.835 --> 00:06:56.835
I feel that, you know, having
first bought books for Korean back

00:06:56.835 --> 00:06:59.295
in 1973, so that's a long time.

00:06:59.295 --> 00:07:00.705
I've had those books a long time.

00:07:00.705 --> 00:07:03.015
I've put a lot of effort into Korean.

00:07:03.285 --> 00:07:06.675
If I were to open up some of these books
that are on my bookshelf, you'll see

00:07:06.675 --> 00:07:10.755
that there's underlinings and tick marks
and all kinds of things, but you know,

00:07:10.755 --> 00:07:14.745
you need to have meaningful, interesting
content that you can stay with.

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:19.060
Which I have for Persian right now. That
you're not deliberately trying to learn.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:21.940
I was too much deliberately
trying to learn the language

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:24.160
rather than enjoying the language.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:27.400
Once you get to where you can
enjoy the language, even if the

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.160
progress is slow, it's enjoyable.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:34.040
I got to where the Korean
wasn't enjoyable, it was work.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:39.170
And when it's work, you know, I
find that it's harder to commit the

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:41.870
time necessary to be successful.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:47.480
So I'm gonna end up here with showing
you me working on Korean at LingQ.

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:51.950
And also after that you'll see a
video, as I say, of a conversation

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:55.670
I had about nine years ago after
one of my 90-day challenges.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:08:00.200
So I haven't given up on Korean, but it's
been a long time and I haven't achieved

00:08:00.414 --> 00:08:02.245
what I would have liked to have achieved.

00:08:02.245 --> 00:08:02.995
Thanks for listening.

00:08:03.145 --> 00:08:03.625
Bye for now.

00:08:05.385 --> 00:08:09.615
Yeah, I just wanna show you, you
know, how I study on LingQ here.

00:08:09.675 --> 00:08:11.925
I'm going to use the computer version.

00:08:11.925 --> 00:08:13.635
I could also do it on my iPad.

00:08:13.695 --> 00:08:19.005
You can see here that our Korean page has
improved tremendously, so we now have so

00:08:19.005 --> 00:08:21.855
much content here to choose from. Literary,

00:08:22.025 --> 00:08:27.125
audio books, ab workout, you
know, all kinds of different stuff.

00:08:27.515 --> 00:08:31.565
Getting started, you know, we have our
Mini Stories, which we never had before.

00:08:31.565 --> 00:08:33.454
To that extent, it's a lot better.

00:08:33.515 --> 00:08:37.235
I can even select the level that I want
to be looking at in terms of content.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:38.939
But I'll give you an example.

00:08:38.939 --> 00:08:40.470
So here's this thing I found.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:42.900
This is imported from YouTube.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.450
We now have the ability to
import things from YouTube,

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.370
which wasn't the case before.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.280
So here's a lesson that
I found in our library.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:55.319
I could have gone to YouTube to
find it myself, but at any rate,

00:08:55.325 --> 00:08:57.150
so I can click on this thing.

00:08:57.330 --> 00:08:57.900
Okay.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:03.300
So it's kind of childish, but
something I might do. Okay, if I go here.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:09.479
So I can listen to the audio
just for that sentence.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:13.560
I can look up individual words, which I
could do before, but it's much faster.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.969
So I have all these different
meanings, with the most likely

00:09:17.969 --> 00:09:20.280
one up at the top. Show translation.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.770
Now, if it becomes more of
a sentence, you'll see here.

00:09:31.095 --> 00:09:34.515
So this is not exactly fascinating
content, but it just shows you

00:09:34.845 --> 00:09:38.505
how LingQ has improved with the
introduction of, you know,

00:09:38.505 --> 00:09:40.125
the ability to import content.

00:09:40.305 --> 00:09:42.645
And I'm gonna show you
this, but you can see that you

00:09:42.645 --> 00:09:49.065
can import MP3 files, eBooks,
lessons, so I can bring material in.

00:09:49.425 --> 00:09:54.975
If I import an Audio Transcribe, the
Whisper AI will give me a transcript

00:09:54.975 --> 00:09:56.595
so that I will create a lesson from it.

00:09:56.865 --> 00:10:00.975
So there's so many different types of
content that I can use that the whole

00:10:00.975 --> 00:10:04.365
experience has become much more agreeable.

00:10:08.505 --> 00:10:15.105
If I go to a website like Chosun Ilbo
here, and they've had floods in Korea,

00:10:15.105 --> 00:10:19.725
and if I pick an article about the
floods like this one, and I go to our

00:10:19.785 --> 00:10:26.040
web browser, our browser extension, and
I can import it. And it'll come into

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:28.080
LingQ so I can study it as a lesson.

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:31.740
All right, we'll just wait a
few seconds while it comes in.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:36.240
And I... one of the things that has
changed compared to previously is the

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:41.190
tremendous availability of things that
I can import with a click, using our

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:45.600
browser extension. From YouTube, from
articles in newspapers, and so forth.

00:10:45.605 --> 00:10:46.710
I open up the lesson.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:47.640
I have a lesson.

00:10:48.345 --> 00:10:48.824
All right.

00:10:48.855 --> 00:10:52.755
You'll notice that there
is a lot of blue text.

00:10:52.785 --> 00:10:54.525
These are unknown words.

00:10:55.095 --> 00:11:01.935
I have about 13,000 words, known
words in Persian, and if I go import

00:11:02.025 --> 00:11:05.954
something into LingQ, you will
see that there are far fewer new

00:11:05.954 --> 00:11:12.824
words, having spent far less time on
Persian, I understand much more of it.

00:11:12.824 --> 00:11:13.275
Look at this.

00:11:13.275 --> 00:11:14.595
There are just two blue words here.

00:11:15.035 --> 00:11:20.314
You can see that in the case of
Persian, you know, if I go to

00:11:20.314 --> 00:11:26.015
my all time numbers here, I've been at
it since 2018 and kind of like a peak

00:11:26.704 --> 00:11:28.865
known words addition is like 1,000.

00:11:29.355 --> 00:11:31.785
And that's a peak most of the time.

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:33.135
It's quite a bit less than that.

00:11:33.285 --> 00:11:39.375
If I go and look at my Korean situation,
despite the amount of time that I spent

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:43.755
on it, you know, and, and you can see
it goes back to 2010, and there are days

00:11:43.755 --> 00:11:47.715
where I hit like 4,000 known words added.

00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:51.589
Now I'm helped by the fact that
I know Chinese, and so I can plow

00:11:51.589 --> 00:11:55.220
forward in Korean. And I will add
more and more known words, but

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:56.480
it'll still be difficult for me.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:00.140
So I have this greater sense of
just working and working and not

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:05.329
getting anywhere in Korean,
as opposed to in Persian

